## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of transactional memory in real workloads, careful fallback design, and low-latency queues.

## Key Concepts
- Intel TSX (_xbegin/_xend)
- CAS-based fallback paths
- Per-slot sequence counters to avoid ABA
- Cache-line alignment and false sharing avoidance
- Microbenchmarking for latency and throughput

## Code Example
```c
// Skeleton TSX-based enqueue
typedef struct { unsigned long long seq; unsigned long long val; } slot_t;
typedef struct { slot_t *slots; unsigned head, tail, cap; } q_t;
static inline int enqueue_htm(q_t *q, unsigned long long val) {
    unsigned pos = q->tail;
    if ((pos + 1) % q->cap == q->head) return -1; // full

    unsigned status = _xbegin();
    if (status == _XBEGIN_STARTED) {
        q->slots[pos].val = val;
        q->slots[pos].seq = pos + 1;
        q->tail = (pos + 1) % q->cap;
        _xend();
        return 0;
    }

    // Fallback: CAS-based enqueue with sequence validation
    return enqueue_cas(q, val);
}
```

## Design Considerations
- Retry limits: 3-5 TSX attempts before permanent fallback
- Alignment: 64-byte cache line boundaries
- Contention handling: exponential backoff in fallback path
- Benchmark: measure 99th percentile latency under 1 vs 16 producer/consumer pairs

## Why This Is Asked
Low-level memory management on constrained hardware.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size allocator (slab)
- Free-list stored inside blocks; index-based chaining
- O(1) allocations/deallocations with minimal metadata
- Alignment, memory overhead, and fragmentation considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct {
  uint32_t next; // index of next free slot
} FreeSlot;

#define POOL_SIZE 256
#define SLOT_SIZE 64
static uint8_t pool[POOL_SIZE * SLOT_SIZE];
static uint32_t head = 0; // index of first free slot
static uint32_t used = 0;

void slab_init(void) {
  for (uint32_t i = 0; i < POOL_SIZE - 1; ++i) {
    ((uint32_t*)pool)[i] = i + 1;
  }
  ((uint32_t*)pool)[POOL_SIZE - 1] = UINT32_MAX;
  head = 0;
  used = 0;
}

void* slab_alloc(void) {
  if (head == UINT32_MAX) return NULL;
  uint32_t idx = head;
  head = ((uint32_t*)pool)[idx];
  ++used;
  return pool + idx * SLOT_SIZE;
}

void slab_free(void* p) {
  ptrdiff_t idx = ((uint8_t*)p - pool) / SLOT_SIZE;
  ((uint32_t*)pool)[idx] = head;
  head = (uint32_t)idx;
  --used;
}
```

